Subject: Modeling of spatial datasets
Message-ID: <A6Yp2WNPs_8.ouf28kDtDQAJ@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/r-inla-discussion-group/c/A6Yp2WNPs_8/m/ouf28kDtDQAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Dear INLA community,

I want to a spatial dataset. The data set is of form (x,y;z), where (x,y) are the coordinates, and z is the associated observation at that location.
Basically, suppose there ia a true surface (x,y;z0), then the error/uncertainty in the observation z can be roughly divided into two parts: a, which is independent of (x,y;z0) and b, which is depending on z0 saying e.g. 2% of z0.
This should be a quite common assumption in geology that some uncertainty depend on the values themselves. I don't know whether/how can INLA implement such modeling? Thanks in advance!

Best regards,
David

